## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design per-tenant budgets, dynamic throttling, and robust rollback strategies in a multi-tenant, latency-sensitive service.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant SLOs and budgets
- Burn rate calculation and budget ledger
- Edge throttling (token bucket) and graceful degradation
- Canary rollouts and automated rollback
- Observability with per-tenant metrics and PromQL

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: update per-tenant budget on each request
function onRequest(req){
  const tenant = req.headers['tenant-id'];
  const now = Date.now();
  const budget = ledger.getBudget(tenant);
  if (budget.remaining <= 0) throttle(req, tenant);
  else { forward(req); ledger.consume(tenant, estimateCost(req)); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this system under simulated traffic bursts?
- What thresholds would you choose for alerting and auto-rollback cadence?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design scalable alerting that reduces noise while preserving critical signals, across multi-region K8s clusters, using common tools, with safe rollout.

## Key Concepts
- Alert routing and deduplication (Alertmanager, labels)
- Global blackout policy with per-region windows
- Canary rollout for rule changes; staging validation
- Metrics: alert rate, MTTA, MTTR, missed-incident risk; rollback plan

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple in-memory alert suppression
const suppression = new Map(); // key -> timestamp until which alerts are muted
function shouldAlert(key) {
  const t = suppression.get(key);
  if (!t) return true;
  return Date.now() > t;
}
function suppress(key, durationMs) {
  suppression.set(key, Date.now() + durationMs);
}
```

